// _tests_/bit/benchgate/baselineslinux.bit — the two Linux baseline tables for
// the benchmark regression gate, split out of `baselines.bit` when #3934's
// second alloc case took that file over the 800-line limit (E0200). A pure
// move: `baselinesLinuxX64` and `baselinesLinuxArm64` are here VERBATIM,
// every measurement and calibration comment intact, and `baselinesFor` still
// calls both from `baselines.bit` — sibling files in one directory module
// share a scope, so nothing is imported and no call site changed.
// Measured on real x86_64-linux hardware (hl-master, i7-6700@3.4GHz, 6
// vCPUs) via `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master STEP=test-benchgate bash
// scripts/x64gate.sh fast 2`. Every figure below is the minimum of at least
// two independent runs, each itself benchgate's own min of `reps` (3) — the
// same "minimum cannot be inflated by noise" reasoning the file header gives
// for `reps`. Provenance is per row, and each row's comment carries its own
// readings: collatz/mandelbrot/fib on main 6d315775 (#4726), alloc on main
// 306e0aef (#2936). #2932/#2933's original cold-build pair is superseded on
// all four rows; those numbers live in those tickets, not here.
// The ratio to the macOS/arm64 table above is NOT constant across cases
// (1.7x collatz, 1.7x mandelbrot, 2.2x fib, 2.5x alloc — re-derived at
// 6d315775 against that table's 29/121/29/82cs, replacing the 3.5-5.0x
// spread #2932's numbers gave) — a pure host-speed difference would be
// roughly constant, so part of the gap is x86_64 codegen quality, not clock
// speed alone (filed separately, not chased here). RSS on this host is
// LOWER than macOS/arm64 (Linux's /proc VmHWM accounting differs from
// macOS's `/usr/bin/time -l`), well under its own ceiling.
// objects (#3130) is RECORDED as 2 on collatz, mandelbrot and fib: both
// #4726 runs printed obj=2 for each of the three, byte-identical, which is
// the determinism #3130's two-sided band needs. lxCaseAlloc keeps -1 ("not
// measured on this platform") — #2936's two runs both printed obj=10006002
// but left it unrecorded, and #4726's scope did not include that row, so
// recording it needs its own ticket rather than a paste from another one.
// The band check in benchgate.bit's runCase skips a -1 baseline; the
// BIT_GC_STATS line is still required unconditionally regardless.
// #3615: baselinesMacosArm64() above was bumped +2 per case for #2579's
// sysmon monitor thread, a fixed boot-path allocation. This table did not
// get that bump, because #2579's sysmon thread is DARWIN-ONLY
// (`sched/darwin/`), and #4726's measurement is consistent with that: obj=2
// on all three cases here against macOS/arm64's 6, the same 2-vs-6 gap
// baselinesLinuxArm64() below records. The gap is 4, so it is not the
// sysmon +2 alone; what the other 2 are has not been measured here and is
// not claimed.
// Re-measured on main 6d315775 by #4726, replacing #2932's 327cs/229376B:
// two independent runs of `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master STEP=test-benchgate bash
// scripts/x64gate.sh fast 2`, each printing its own min of `reps` (3) —
//   run 1: collatz cpu=48cs rss=208896B obj=2
//   run 2: collatz cpu=48cs rss=204800B obj=2
// — 0cs spread on CPU, one 4096B page on RSS, identical object count. The
// minimum of the two is recorded, this table's convention. The old figure
// was 6.8x the real cost, so its 1.5x ceiling sat at 490cs against a 48cs
// case: a 10x regression would have PASSed. X64GATE_PEERS=0 and
// X64LINUX_EXIT=0 on both runs; host `vmstat 1 5` 83-90% idle on 6 vCPUs
// before and 83-90% after, top non-benchmark process tailscaled at 1.0%,
// one ssh login, no peer container. cpuCeilingPct stays at the blanket 150:
// no false positive has been observed on this platform.
fn lxCaseCollatz(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "collatz",
    expected: "524",
    cpuCentis: 48,
    rssBytes: 204800,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 2,
  }
}

// #3173: this row's `expected` and `cpuCentis` were UNVALIDATED -- both
// carried over from the macOS/arm64 row above without ever compiling and
// running on this platform. `expected: "204866964"` happened to be
// right, but only by luck of timing: until #3168 landed (451c9993,
// "partition the spill pool by register class -- fixes an all-platform
// parallel-move miscompile shipped in 0.1.18"), x86_64 genuinely printed
// a different, wrong answer for this case (726141000, i.e. 3.54x the
// correct summed-iteration count) -- so this row could never have PASSed
// on its own hardware before that fix landed. `cpuCentis: 559` was never
// a measurement of this binary either: it was close to the arm64 row's
// 121cs by the family's ~4.6x ratio (coincidence, not derivation) and
// nowhere near the ~5519cs the miscompiled path actually cost (that
// number is recorded in #3173's own ticket comments, not here, because
// recording a baseline for a miscompiled binary would have ratcheted in
// the wrong figure).
//
// RE-VERIFIED on hl-master (i7-6700, real x86_64 Linux) after #3168,
// 2026-08-17. Host checked idle before and throughout: load average
// 2.6-4.4 on 6 vCPUs (own containers' own load, not contention), %CPU
// idle 85-92% via vmstat, top non-benchmark process ~1% CPU
// (tailscaled), single ssh login. `expected: "204866964"` is now
// CONFIRMED correct -- not carried over, observed: 8 direct runs of the
// exact harness `./make test-benchgate` runs (`BIT_STDLIB=... BIT_BIN=...
// BIT_LIBBITRT=... BIT_BENCH_DIR=... bit-out/bin/bit run
// _tests_/bit/benchgate`), invoked directly rather than through
// `scripts/x64gate.sh` end-to-end because, at the time of this
// measurement, #3227 -- a bug where the batch driver's watchdog
// subshell used bash-only `exec -a`, which dash (this image's /bin/sh)
// rejects -- killed every gate within ~1s of starting regardless of
// the actual work. #3227 (`tools/build/gates.bit`, merged a8e0b012)
// and its duplicate #3231 (`_tests_/bit/importsrun/importsrun.bit`, merged
// b105f8a3) have since landed, so `x64gate.sh` end-to-end works on a
// dash host again -- confirmed 2026-08-17 (#3235) by rerunning
// `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master STEP=test-benchgate bash scripts/x64gate.sh`
// end-to-end: `X64LINUX_EXIT=0`, `benchgate: PASS`. All 8 direct runs
// printed the byte-identical correct value and PASSed (same assertion
// code `./make test-benchgate` runs). cpuCentis
// readings across those 8 runs: 267 278 279 283 284 285 291 291 --
// minimum 267cs, a 24cs (9%) spread -- replacing 559 below, the same
// "minimum cannot be inflated by noise" convention this file's other
// baselines use.
//
// RE-MEASURED again on main 6d315775 by #4726, replacing that 267cs (which
// was #3173's direct-harness minimum, taken before x64gate.sh worked end to
// end on this host): two independent runs of `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master
// STEP=test-benchgate bash scripts/x64gate.sh fast 2`, each its own min of
// `reps` (3) —
//   run 1: mandelbrot cpu=203cs rss=212992B obj=2
//   run 2: mandelbrot cpu=203cs rss=208896B obj=2
// — 0cs spread, one 4096B page on RSS, identical object count; minimum
// recorded. `expected: "204866964"` is unchanged and printed correctly on
// both runs. X64GATE_PEERS=0, X64LINUX_EXIT=0 both runs; host 83-90% idle
// by `vmstat 1 5` before and after, tailscaled 1.0% the top non-benchmark
// process, one ssh login, no peer container.
fn lxCaseMandelbrot(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "mandelbrot",
    expected: "204866964",
    cpuCentis: 203,
    rssBytes: 208896,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 2,
  }
}

// Re-measured on main 6d315775 by #4726, replacing #2932's 101cs/225280B:
// three independent runs of `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master STEP=test-benchgate
// bash scripts/x64gate.sh`, each its own min of `reps` (3) —
//   run 1: fib cpu=69cs rss=204800B obj=2
//   run 2: fib cpu=69cs rss=204800B obj=2
//   run 3: fib cpu=64cs rss=204800B obj=2
// Run 3 is #4726's mutation-proof invocation, one commit later, which
// changed only lxCaseCollatz's cpuCeilingPct and therefore measured the
// identical fib binary. Spread 5cs (7.2%) on CPU, byte-identical RSS and
// object count. The MINIMUM of the three is recorded, this table's
// convention, so this row is 64cs and not the 69cs the first two runs
// agreed on; the 1.5x ceiling that gives (96cs) still sits 27cs above the
// highest reading observed. X64GATE_PEERS=0 on all three, X64LINUX_EXIT=0
// on runs 1-2 (run 3 was the deliberate FAIL); host 83-90% idle by
// `vmstat 1 5` before every run, tailscaled 1.0% the top non-benchmark
// process, one ssh login, no peer container.
//
// #2982's fib fix still does NOT transfer here -- deliberately left at the
// blanket 150 (1.5x). The defect on macOS/arm64 is an ABSOLUTE-headroom
// problem: baseline 29cs, 1.5x ceiling 43cs, only 14cs of absolute
// headroom for scheduling noise that is itself roughly fixed in
// centiseconds, not proportional to the baseline -- that 14cs is what
// real contention filled. hl-master's fib baseline is 64cs (2.2x larger),
// so 1.5x gives 96cs, i.e. 32cs of absolute headroom -- 2.3x the margin
// that broke on macOS/arm64, against a spread of 5cs across #4726's three
// runs. Raising this to 190 would buy nothing against a mechanism this
// baseline is not exposed to, while costing real detection power: it would
// move the ceiling from 96cs to 121cs against a 2x regression at 128cs,
// shrinking the margin from 32cs to 7cs -- reintroducing the thin-boundary
// problem #2982 fixed on macOS/arm64, on the one platform with no direct
// load measurement to justify it. If a false positive is ever observed on
// hl-master, that needs its own ticket with fresh measurements from that
// hardware, not a number inferred from this box.
fn lxCaseFib(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "fib",
    expected: "102334155",
    cpuCentis: 64,
    rssBytes: 204800,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 2,
  }
}

// #3934 changed what this case allocates on EVERY platform, so
// `expected` moves here too: 59995000000 -> 69990000000, each node now
// summing its batch id as well. The value is platform-independent (it is
// the program's own arithmetic, and the closed form gives it
// independently), so recording it here is a fact, not a guess.
//
// cpuCentis/rssBytes below ARE now measured for that shape, on main
// 306e0aef, replacing the inherited #2932/#2933 pair (1144cs/8749056B)
// that was taken on the pre-#3862 boxed program: two independent runs of
// `X64GATE_HOST=hl-master STEP=test-benchgate bash scripts/x64gate.sh
// fast 2`, each printing its own min of `reps` (3) repetitions --
//   run 1: alloc cpu=208cs rss=4734976B
//   run 2: alloc cpu=208cs rss=4739072B
// -- so 0cs spread on CPU and one 4096B page on RSS; the minimum of the
// two is recorded, the convention the rest of this table uses.
// X64GATE_PEERS=0 and X64LINUX_EXIT=0 on both. Host checked idle before
// and after by `vmstat 1 5` (84-90% idle, 6 vCPUs; the 3.2 load average
// on this box is its own containers' I/O, the same reading #3173
// recorded) with the top non-benchmark process at 1.0% (tailscaled), one
// ssh login. objects stays -1: measured obj=10006002 on both runs but
// left unrecorded here. That reason was "the other three entries are
// unmeasured too"; #4726 measured them and recorded obj=2 on each, so the
// reason is now simply that #4726's scope excluded this row -- recording
// 10006002 needs a ticket that measures it, not a paste (see the objects
// paragraph in the header above).
fn lxCaseAlloc(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "alloc",
    expected: "69990000000",
    cpuCentis: 208,
    rssBytes: 4734976,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: -1,
  }
}

// bench/cases/allocflat (#3934) is deliberately ABSENT from this table:
// never measured on this platform, and omitted rather than guessed --
// the same precedent #2978's churn case set five lines below.
// `baselinesFor`'s per-platform length is what makes that safe.
// #2978's churn case has NOT been measured on this platform (no Linux
// host in this session) -- omitted rather than guessed, matching
// baselinesLinuxArm64's precedent below. baselinesFor's per-platform
// table length means this is safe: main()'s completion check compares
// against len(table), not a hardcoded case count.
fn baselinesLinuxX64(): []Baseline {
  return [
    lxCaseCollatz(), lxCaseMandelbrot(), lxCaseFib(), lxCaseAlloc(),
  ]
}

// linux/aarch64, measured on main 306e0aef with `ARM64GATE_STEP=test-benchgate
// bash scripts/arm64gate.sh fast 2` — arm64gate.sh spells its step override
// ARM64GATE_STEP, not the bare STEP x64gate.sh reads. Native aarch64 image
// under Docker on this Apple-Silicon host, so real ARM64 Linux, no qemu; both
// runs held the WHOLE box via `.claude/boxlock.sh solo`, ARM64GATE_PEERS=0 on
// each, ARM64GATE_LOAD 2.76 before and 2.58 after. Each figure below is the
// min of two independent runs, each itself the min of `reps` (3) repetitions:
//   run 1: collatz 20cs/2285568B obj=2, mandelbrot 46cs/2285568B obj=2,
//          fib 20cs/2289664B obj=2, alloc 35cs/7110656B obj=10006002
//   run 2: collatz 20cs/2285568B obj=2, mandelbrot 46cs/2285568B obj=2,
//          fib 20cs/2285568B obj=2, alloc 34cs/7110656B obj=10006002
// Spread: 0cs on three cases, 1cs (2.9%) on alloc; RSS identical but for one
// 4096B page on fib. `objects` is RECORDED here rather than -1 because both
// runs agreed exactly on every case, which is the determinism #3130's
// two-sided band needs; it is NOT the macOS/arm64 count (6 and 10006006), and
// that gap is darwin-only exactly as #3615 predicted. cpuCeilingPct stays at
// the blanket 150 on all four: no false positive has been observed on this
// platform, and #2982's fib note is explicit that widening needs one measured
// on the hardware rather than inferred from another box.
fn lxaCaseCollatz(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "collatz",
    expected: "524",
    cpuCentis: 20,
    rssBytes: 2285568,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 2,
  }
}

fn lxaCaseMandelbrot(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "mandelbrot",
    expected: "204866964",
    cpuCentis: 46,
    rssBytes: 2285568,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 2,
  }
}

fn lxaCaseFib(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "fib",
    expected: "102334155",
    cpuCentis: 20,
    rssBytes: 2285568,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 2,
  }
}

fn lxaCaseAlloc(): Baseline {
  return Baseline{
    name: "alloc",
    expected: "69990000000",
    cpuCentis: 34,
    rssBytes: 7110656,
    gcMinKb: 0,
    cpuCeilingPct: 150,
    objects: 10006002,
  }
}

// bench/cases/{allocflat,churn} are ABSENT here for the same reason they are
// absent from baselinesLinuxX64() above: not measured on this platform,
// omitted rather than guessed. baselinesFor()'s per-platform table length is
// what makes that safe.
fn baselinesLinuxArm64(): []Baseline {
  return [
    lxaCaseCollatz(), lxaCaseMandelbrot(), lxaCaseFib(), lxaCaseAlloc(),
  ]
}
